window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][14]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M14-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M14-L1 – MOSFET Models for Circuit Design<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Microelectronic Circuits and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Transistors<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Semiconductors<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Network\nanalysis<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate a large-signal MOSFET\nmodel, identify its regions of operation and derive a local small-signal\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The MOSFET as a Controllable Channel<\/h2>\n<p>In an integrated circuit, the gate controls the conductivity of a\nchannel between source and drain by means of an electric field. In an\nn-channel enhancement-mode MOSFET, a conducting electron channel forms\nwhen the gate-source voltage is sufficiently positive. The ideally\ninsulated gate electrode draws no steady-state current; current\nnevertheless flows when its capacitances are charged or discharged. A\np-channel transistor operates with reversed polarities; for it, we use\nthe positive magnitudes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{SG}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{SD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|V_{Tp}|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The fourth terminal is the bulk, or body. In the simple model, it is\nconnected to the source. At a different bulk voltage, the body effect\nchanges the threshold voltage; the junctions to the substrate also form\npn diodes. A transistor therefore cannot be used arbitrarily as an ideal\nbidirectional switch in every circuit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Large-Signal Model<\/h2>\n<p>For a long n-channel device, we use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_T=0.35<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=\\mu_n C_{ox}W/L=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA/V². The overdrive is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=V_{GS}-V_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the idealised model,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>q<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>q<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>β<\/mi><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>q<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_D=\\begin{cases}\n0 &amp; q\\le0\\\\\n\\beta(q V_{DS}-V_{DS}^2/2) &amp; 0&lt;V_{DS}&lt;q,\\ q&gt;0\\\\\n\\beta q^2/2 &amp; V_{DS}\\ge q,\\ q&gt;0.\n\\end{cases}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the linear (triode) region, at very small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe approximate resistance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{DS}=1/(\\beta q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the boundary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}=q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe two equations agree. In saturation, the channel is pinched off at\nthe drain end; this does not mean that the current is zero. Integrated\namplifiers exploit this region precisely because the drain voltage has a\nrelatively small influence on the current.<\/p>\n<p>As a simple extension, we multiply the current in the conducting\nregions by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+\\lambda V_{DS})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhere with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a smooth instructional model for output conductance, not a\ncomplete compact process model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_047_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"nMOS output characteristics for four gate voltages, with a dashed boundary between the triode region and saturation.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The modelled nMOS characteristics show the\ntransition from voltage-dependent triode operation to saturation at\ndifferent gate voltages; the dashed line marks the\nboundary.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operating Point and Small-Signal Model<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{GS}=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.45<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0.45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV; the transistor is in saturation. Without the correction, it delivers\n20.25 µA; with the correction, 21.222 µA. At constant bulk voltage and\nfor small variations about this operating point,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta i_d\\approx g_m\\delta v_{gs}+g_{ds}\\delta v_{ds},\\quad\ng_m=\\beta q(1+\\lambda V_{DS}),\\quad g_{ds}=\\lambda\\beta q^2/2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>94.32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m=94.32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµS,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.62<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{ds}=1.62<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµS and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>617<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_o=1/g_{ds}\\approx617<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a dimensionless gain but a transconductance. Voltage gain only\narises in conjunction with an output resistance or load.<\/p>\n<p>Example: a 1.2 V supply and a 20 kΩ drain resistor give the\nsaturation load line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}=1.2-R_D I_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{GS}=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. Substitution of the extended model yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.405<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.0324<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.770<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}=(1.2-0.405)/(1+0.0324)\\approx0.770<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. Checking the region afterwards,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.770<\/mn><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0.45<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.770&gt;0.45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconfirms the assumed equation. Without this check, a formally correct\nequation could yield a physically unsuitable solution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Selection and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The square-law model assumes, among other things, a sufficiently long\nchannel and constant effective mobility. Subthreshold current, velocity\nsaturation, temperature, device variation, and gate and junction\ncapacitances are not included. Real CMOS processes therefore use\nprocess-specific models (such as BSIM) and a symbol library with layout\nrules. The simplified model suffices for an initial functional design;\nit cannot establish manufacturing yield, absolute speed or permissible\noperating voltages.<\/p>\n<p>A numerical test compares the analytical derivatives with central\ndifferences:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>beta, vt, lam <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">200.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.08<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> ids(vg, vd):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(vg<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>vt, <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> beta<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>vd<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>vd<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> vd<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>q <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>lam<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>vd)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>vg, vd <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>gm <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (ids(vg<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h,vd)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ids(vg<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h,vd))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>gds <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (ids(vg,vd<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ids(vg,vd<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(gm<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">94.32<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(gds<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.62<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(ids(vg,vd), gm, gds)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The current values are in µA, so the derivatives are in µS. Agreement\nbetween the derivatives checks the implementation, not agreement with\nsilicon.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Identify the region for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.55<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{GS}=0.55<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, triode region; without the output correction,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.005<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_D=200(0.02-0.005)=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis doubled and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unchanged?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> It doubles; the gate capacitance also\nincreases in the simple area model. More current comes at the cost of\nchip area and charging energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is a low\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{DS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nautomatically advantageous for a high-impedance amplifier output?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> No. The switch model applies near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin amplifier operation, a high output resistance in saturation is often\ndesirable.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">CMOS\ninverters and static logic<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW 6.012 – Lectures 10–12: MOSFET models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lectures 10–12: MOSFET\noperating regions and drain-current models; lecture table only. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M14-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M14-L2 – CMOS Inverters and Static Logic<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Microelectronic Circuits and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Boolean\nlogic<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">MOSFET\nmodels<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the static transfer\ncharacteristic of a CMOS inverter and explain the switching threshold,\nnoise margins and logic gate networks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complementary Pull-up and Pull-down Networks<\/h2>\n<p>A CMOS inverter has a pMOS between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the output, and an nMOS between the output and ground. Their gates\nare connected to the same input. When the input is low, the pMOS\nconducts and the nMOS is off; the output is charged to the supply\nvoltage. When the input is high, their roles are reversed. In the ideal\nstatic model, the gate of a subsequent logic gate does not place a DC\ncurrent load on the output.<\/p>\n<p>Logical inversion is not equivalent to linear amplification. Between\nthe two states, both transistors may conduct. In this region, the output\ndepends on the current balance. In real transistors, leakage currents\nremain even outside the transition region.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transfer Characteristic and Switching Threshold<\/h2>\n<p>We use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{Tn}=|V_{Tp}|=0.35<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_n=\\beta_p=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA/V². The characteristic is obtained by solving<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_n(V_{in},V_{out})=I_p(V_{DD}-V_{in},V_{DD}-V_{out}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The pMOS current is defined as a positive magnitude flowing towards\nthe output. At the intersection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{out}=V_{in}=V_M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nboth transistors are in saturation for positive overdrives. Neglecting\noutput conductance, the equation can be solved for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_M=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\beta_p}(V_{DD}-|V_{Tp}|)+\\sqrt{\\beta_n}V_{Tn}}{\\sqrt{\\beta_p}+\\sqrt{\\beta_n}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The symmetrical model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_M=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved, the transition moves towards lower input voltages\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.557<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_M\\approx0.557<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV without the correction). Transistor drive strength is not determined\nby geometrical width alone: pMOS and nMOS have different mobilities in\nmany processes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_048_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Static output voltage versus input voltage for both inverters, with the slope criterion points and diagonal marked.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The two inverter transfer curves have different\nswitching regions and slopes; the marked limits illustrate static noise\nmargins.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Noise Margins<\/h2>\n<p>The points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare determined on the falling transfer characteristic where the slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dV_{out}/dV_{in}=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor compatible identical inverters, we conservatively set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OH}=V_{out}(V_{IL})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OL}=V_{out}(V_{IH})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe static noise margins are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NM_L=V_{IL}-V_{OL},\\qquad NM_H=V_{OH}-V_{IH}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Using the extended MOS model from L1\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>/V)\ngives approximately the following values. The conservative output levels\nare not the ideal supply rails.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-14\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NM_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NM_H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Symmetrical<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.531 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.669 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.062 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.138 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.469 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.469 V<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weaker pMOS<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.477 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.612 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.067 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.150 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.409 V<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.538 V<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Static noise margins concern DC voltage deviations, not brief noise\npulses, coupling or timing. A smaller low-level noise margin cannot\nautomatically be compensated for by a larger high-level noise\nmargin.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the Inverter to NAND and NOR<\/h2>\n<p>For a two-input NAND, two nMOS devices are connected in series and\ntwo pMOS devices in parallel. A conducting path to ground exists only\nwhen both inputs are high. For a NOR, two nMOS devices are connected in\nparallel and two pMOS devices in series. The complementary networks\nimplement De Morgan’s laws; an AND can be constructed using a NAND and\nan inverter.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">A<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">B<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">NAND<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">NOR<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>In a real cell, the series connection may limit the current and\ntherefore increase delay. Intermediate nodes have their own capacitances\nand voltages, so simply adding constant channel resistances is only an\napproximation. Gate libraries therefore characterise each input and\ntransition direction for several load and edge conditions. A truth table\nalone is insufficient for clock planning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does doubling both\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues leave the static characteristic unchanged in the symmetrical\nmodel?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Both currents scale equally. Their equality\ncondition is unchanged; dynamic currents and capacitances may\nchange.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A receiving gate guarantees a low input up to 0.45 V. A driver\nguarantees a low output of at most 0.12 V. What low-level noise margin\nis available?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> 0.33 V. The limits must be appropriate for the\nconnected logic family and the operating range under consideration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an output voltage of 0.6 V not a valid logic level in the\nsymmetrical model?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> It lies between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthere is no guaranteed logical interpretation. Both transistors may\nconduct.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Dynamics and\npower requirements of integrated circuits<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW 6.012 – Lectures 14–15: Inverters and CMOS<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lectures 14–15:\nCMOS inverter analysis and switching; lecture table only. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://computationstructures.org/notes/cmos/notes.html\">Computation\nStructures – CMOS, static behaviour<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 6: CMOS NAND and NOR\ncircuits; partial scope. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M14-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M14-L3 – Dynamics and Power Requirements of Integrated Circuits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Microelectronic Circuits and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">RC\ntransients<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">CMOS\nlogic<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish and estimate\ncapacitive delays and the dynamic, static and short-circuit power\ncomponents of a CMOS circuit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Charging and Discharging an Output Node<\/h2>\n<p>The static equivalent circuit is extended to include the load\ncapacitance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt comprises the input capacitances of subsequent gates, junction\ncapacitances and interconnect contributions. For fast input switching,\nwe first approximate the conducting transistors by separate resistances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor charging from zero and discharging from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo>↑<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo>↓<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{out,\\uparrow}(t)=V_{DD}(1-e^{-t/(R_p C_L)}),\\qquad\nV_{out,\\downarrow}(t)=V_{DD}e^{-t/(R_n C_L)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The delay between the 50% point of the ideal input transition and the\n50% point of the output is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{pLH}=\\ln(2)R_p C_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{pHL}=\\ln(2)R_n C_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. The rise time from 10 to 90% is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(9)R_p C_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA propagation delay is therefore not the same as a transition time.<\/p>\n<p>Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfF,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_n=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_n=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nns and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_p=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nns;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.139<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{pHL}=0.139<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nns and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.208<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{pLH}=0.208<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nns. The longer 10–90% rise time is 0.659 ns. If the capacitance doubles\nwhile the resistances remain unchanged, these times double.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_049_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"RC charging and discharging curves for 20 and 50 fF, with the 50% point and different channel resistances.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A larger capacitive output load lengthens the\nRC-limited transitions; charging and discharging also differ because of\nthe assumed channel resistances.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy Balance of a Complete Charge Cycle<\/h2>\n<p>When charging from 0 to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe supply delivers the energy<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{source}=\\int V_{DD}i(t)dt=C_L V_{DD}^2,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>of which\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L V_{DD}^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis stored in the capacitor’s electric field. The other half is converted\ninto heat in the charging path. During discharge, the discharge path\ndissipates the stored half. Over a complete voltage swing, the net\nchange in stored energy is zero. The energy demand per 0–1–0 cycle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L V_{DD}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nindependently of the chosen constant resistance, provided that full\ncharging and discharging are achieved and there is no energy\nrecovery.<\/p>\n<p>At 1.2 V and 20 fF, this amounts to 28.8 fJ per cycle. A periodic\nsignal at 100 MHz has exactly one charging event per period: the average\nideal capacitive power is 2.88 µW. At 0.8 V with the same load, it would\nbe 1.28 µW. However, the lower voltage can substantially reduce\ntransistor currents and hence switching speed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Activity Factor and Other Power Components<\/h2>\n<p>In a synchronous system, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote the mean number of 0–1 transitions at node\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper clock period. With this unambiguous convention and full voltage\nswings,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{dyn}=V_{DD}^2 f_{clk}\\sum_i\\alpha_i C_i.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If both edges are counted instead of only the charging edges, a\nfactor of 1/2 is needed for equal numbers of rising and falling edges.\nGlitches can increase activity beyond the number expected from the\nfunctional logic alone.<\/p>\n<p>Example: 1000 nodes of 20 fF each,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nMHz and 1.2 V:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>288<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{dyn}=288<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµW. In addition, short-circuit currents occur when pMOS and nMOS\nconduction overlaps during finite transition times, and leakage currents\ncause static power consumption. An ideal static CMOS gate state is\ntherefore not equivalent to a real lossless chip.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Loads, Interconnects and Design Budget<\/h2>\n<p>For example, a gate drives a fixed interconnect capacitance of 8 fF\nand three identical inputs of 4 fF each. This results in 20 fF. If six\ninputs rather than three are connected, the load is 32 fF; the RC delay\nincreases by a factor of 1.6. In the actual layout, interconnect\nresistance, coupling and distributed capacitances may also dominate. A\nlong conductor can then no longer be usefully modelled as a single\ncapacitor.<\/p>\n<p>A simple timing budget for a synchronous register chain is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{clk}\\ge t_{CQ}+t_{comb}+t_{setup}+t_{reserve}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith 0.3 ns, 2 ns, 0.2 ns and 0.5 ns, the chain requires at least 3 ns\n(at most approximately 333 MHz under the model assumptions). A separate\nhold-time check for the shortest path is also required. Clock skew and\njitter cannot be accounted for solely by an average delay.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{pHL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat 50 fF and 10 kΩ?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nns;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.347<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{pHL}=0.347<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nns.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By how much does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{dyn}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecrease if the voltage changes from 1.2 to 0.9 V while activity,\ncapacitance and frequency remain unchanged?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.9/1.2)^2=0.5625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n43.75% lower. Whether the original frequency remains achievable must be\nchecked separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L V_{DD}^2 f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot be used as an exact result for an incomplete voltage swing?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The actual change in charge is smaller and\nshort-circuit current may flow at the same time; the energy must be\nobtained by integrating the product of supply voltage and supply\ncurrent.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Basic\non-chip analogue building blocks<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW 6.012 – Lectures 15–16: Inverter switching delay<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lectures\n15–16 (index): switching delay, power and leakage. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://computationstructures.org/notes/cmos/notes.html\">Computation\nStructures – CMOS, dynamic behaviour<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 6 CMOS, introduction:\nparasitic capacitances, switching and power consumption; qualitative\nrelevance. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M14-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M14-L4 – Basic Analogue Building Blocks at Chip Level<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Microelectronic Circuits and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Analogue\nsignal conditioning<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">MOSFET\nmodels<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Size current mirrors and\ndifferential stages, verify operating regions and assess the trade-offs\nbetween gain, voltage swing and current consumption.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Current Mirrors and Biasing<\/h2>\n<p>In its simplest form, an nMOS current mirror consists of a transistor\nwith its gate and drain connected and a second transistor at the same\ngate potential. The source terminals are grounded. The reference current\nsets the gate-source voltage. For both transistors in saturation,\nneglecting output conductance and assuming equal threshold voltages,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{I_{out}}{I_{ref}}=\\frac{\\beta_2}{\\beta_1}=\\frac{(W/L)_2}{(W/L)_1}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{ref}=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_1=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA/V², the ideal overdrive is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.447<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\sqrt{2 I_{ref}/\\beta_1}=0.447<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.797<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{GS}=0.797<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. A second transistor with twice the width-to-length ratio ideally\nmirrors 40 µA. It requires a drain voltage of at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto do so. Below this voltage it leaves saturation and the current falls.\nThis minimum voltage headroom is referred to as the compliance\nvoltage.<\/p>\n<p>Including output conductance, the mirror ratio is corrected for a\nknown common gate voltage and equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{I_{out}}{I_{ref}}=\\frac{\\beta_2}{\\beta_1}\\frac{1+\\lambda V_{DS2}}{1+\\lambda V_{DS1}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{GS}=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS1}=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS2}=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>/V,\nthe ratio for twice the width is approximately 1.955 rather than 2. Here\nthe gate voltage is prescribed and the reference current follows from\nthe model. Small geometry and threshold-voltage mismatches further\nreduce mirror accuracy. Cascoding improves output resistance but\nrequires additional voltage headroom.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differential Amplifier<\/h2>\n<p>Two identical nMOS transistors with connected source terminals share\nan ideal tail current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet the gate voltages be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{G1}=V_{CM}+v_d/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{G2}=V_{CM}-v_d/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach transistor carries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_T/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn increasing differential signal raises one branch current and lowers\nthe other; the sum remains constant. We assume identical transistors in\nsaturation and initially set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenoting the mean of the two overdrive voltages, the sum of the\nsquare-law currents gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_T=\\beta(q^2+v_d^2/4),\\qquad q=\\sqrt{I_T/\\beta-v_d^2/4}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>As long as both transistors conduct and remain in saturation,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1-I_2=\\beta v_d\\sqrt{I_T/\\beta-v_d^2/4}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|v_d|=\\sqrt{2I_T/\\beta}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none of the branch currents reaches zero. Beyond this point the entire\ncurrent flows through the other branch, provided its saturation\ncondition still holds. A differential pair limits large input\ndifferences rather than amplifying them linearly without bound.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_050_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Idealised branch currents of a differential pair and their difference at a total current of 40 µA, including the full current-steering limits.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The idealized differential input voltage\nredistributes a fixed tail current between two MOS branches; at large\ndifferences nearly all current moves to one branch.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gain and Voltage Headroom<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_T=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA/V² per branch. At the symmetrical operating point,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.447<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\sqrt{I_T/\\beta}=0.447<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>89.44<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m=\\beta q=89.44<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµS. Two equal drain resistors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_D=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ connected to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV each yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_D=1.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.95<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{CM}=0.95<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, the common source is at 0.153 V in the ideal model. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe input MOSFETs are in saturation with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.847<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DS}=0.847<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. A real tail current source would, however, have to operate at just\n0.153 V; this is precisely one of the design challenges at low supply\nvoltages.<\/p>\n<p>For the differential output signal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{od}=v_{o1}-v_{o2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe approximate gain is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.894<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_d=\\frac{v_{od}}{v_d}\\approx -g_mR_D=-0.894.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A single-ended output taken from one branch would ideally have half\nthe magnitude of the differential gain. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV, the large-signal formula gives currents of approximately 22.233 µA\nand 17.767 µA; the output difference is −44.65 mV. This agrees closely\nwith the small-signal calculation. The formal current distribution for\nlarge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not yet guarantee that both drain voltages remain within the\npermissible range.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interface Between Analogue and Digital Circuits<\/h2>\n<p>A differential stage is at the heart of many input amplifiers and\ncomparators. A precision linear amplifier requires sufficient loop gain,\nbandwidth and suitable negative feedback. A comparator, by contrast, is\nusually designed for rapid decisions and defined output levels; it\ncannot be used as an operational amplifier without verification.<\/p>\n<p>Active loads can provide high output resistance with a smaller static\nvoltage drop. Higher output impedance and node capacitance, however,\nproduce a longer time constant. Increasing transistor area can reduce\nrandom mismatch but increases capacitances. A higher bias current\nincreases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut consumes more power. The idealised pair above draws\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2\\cdot40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA = 48 µW from the supply (excluding separate reference branches).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can an ideal mirror produce 10 µA from 20 µA?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(W/L)_2/(W/L)_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis 0.5. The output still requires sufficient drain voltage.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the differential current at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_T=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Zero; both branches carry 20 µA each.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What differential small-signal gain would result from 20 kΩ instead\nof 10 kΩ?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Formally −1.789. However, the quiescent drain\nvoltage drops to 0.8 V; output headroom and saturation conditions must\nbe checked again.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Design and\nsimulation case study<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW 6.012 – Lectures 17 and 19–20: current mirrors, differential\namplifiers, active loads<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lectures 17–21 (index): analog amplifier\nstages and current mirrors. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M14-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M14-L5 – Design and Simulation Case Study<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Microelectronic Circuits and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">MOSFETs<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Noise\nmargins<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Switching\ndynamics<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Numerical\nintegration<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Simulate a nonlinear inverter\nnumerically and derive a suitable operating range from signal levels,\nload, frequency and energy requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Design Task and Model Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>An output driver is to drive a subsequent digital block. The design\nis investigated using a synthetic CMOS model rather than a specific\nmanufacturing process. The available design variables are supply\nvoltage, external load capacitance and input frequency. Both transistors\nin the model have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµA/V²,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|V_T|=0.35<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.08<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>/V.\nWe use the extended large-signal model from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">L1<\/a>,\nomitting gate, Miller and temperature-dependent capacitances as well as\nsubthreshold currents. The load capacitance is constant.<\/p>\n<p>At the end of each half of the input period, the task requires an\noutput level no higher than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1V_{DD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor no lower than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9<\/mn><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9V_{DD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. These self-imposed criteria are stricter than merely\ncrossing the switching threshold. They are neither a technology\nspecification nor a complete setup/hold verification. The input voltage\nrises and falls linearly within 0.05 ns each; the two transitions begin\nat 0 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nonlinear Differential Equation and Energy<\/h2>\n<p>At the output node, taking the positive current direction from the\npMOS to the node and from the node to the nMOS, respectively,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L\\dot v=I_p(V_{DD}-V_{in},V_{DD}-v)-I_n(V_{in},v).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndrawn from the supply and the energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndissipated in the two conducting channels can be integrated\nsimultaneously as two additional states:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot E_s=V_{DD}I_p,\\qquad \\dot E_d=vI_n+(V_{DD}-v)I_p.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Multiplying the node equation by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirectly gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>v<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot E_s-\\dot E_d=C_Lv\\dot v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus, for any time interval,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_s-\\Delta E_d=\\frac{C_L}{2}(v_{Ende}^2-v_{Start}^2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here the original subscripts Ende and Start denote the end and\nbeginning of the interval. In periodic steady state, the right-hand side\nvanishes. The energy balance is also valid for a period that has not yet\nreached steady state. As the model uses current in µA, capacitance in fF\nand time in ns, µA/fF = V/ns and µA·V·ns = fJ. Average power in fJ/ns\ncorresponds to µW. Only the inverter supply branch is included in the\nenergy balance; the external input driver is excluded.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible Simulation<\/h2>\n<p>We integrate using the fourth-order Runge–Kutta method from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L3<\/a>\nand compare two step sizes. The eighth period is evaluated after seven\ncomplete preceding periods. This is a fixed settling time, not a\nmathematical proof of steady state. The code requires Python and NumPy.\nThe static bisection function <code>dc<\/code> can be used separately to\ncheck the equilibrium points from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> sqrt<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> ids(vg, vd, beta<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">200.<\/span>, vt<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>, lam<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.08<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(vg<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>vt,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(vd,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> beta<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>d<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>d<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>d<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>q <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>lam<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>d)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> currents(vin,vout,vdd,bn<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">200<\/span>,bp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">200<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> ids(vin,vout,bn),ids(vdd<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>vin,vdd<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>vout,bp)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> dc(vin,vdd,bp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">200<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    lo,hi<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,vdd<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">65<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(lo<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>hi)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        n,p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>currents(vin,m,vdd,bp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>bp)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> p<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span>n: lo<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>m<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>: hi<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>m<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> (lo<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>hi)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> simulate(vdd<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">20.<\/span>,f<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">100.<\/span>,step<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.002<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    period<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>f <span class=\"co\"># ns<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    ramp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span> <span class=\"co\"># ns<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(period<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>step))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>((n<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">//<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    h<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>period<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> vin(t):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">%<\/span>period<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>ramp: <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> vdd<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>ramp<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>period<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>: <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> vdd<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span>period<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>ramp: <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> vdd<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>period<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>ramp)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> rhs(t,z):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>z[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> i,j<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>currents(vin(t),v,vdd)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.array([(j<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>i)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>c,vdd<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>j,v<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(vdd<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>v)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>j])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([vdd,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> trace<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>n: trace.append((t<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>period,vin(t),<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>z))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>n: <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rhs(t,z)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rhs(t<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,z<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rhs(t<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,z<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rhs(t<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h,z<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>d)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        z<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>d<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>e)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array(trace)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> es<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> ed<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> de<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(x[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(es<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ed<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>de)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-4<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(x[:,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">&gt;=-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-7<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(x[:,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>vdd<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-7<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> x, (x[n<span class=\"op\">//<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],x[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],es,es<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>period,<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(es<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ed<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>de))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-45\"><a href=\"#cb1-45\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-46\"><a href=\"#cb1-46\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cases <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>), (<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>), (<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">500<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-47\"><a href=\"#cb1-47\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         (<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>), (<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">500<\/span>), (<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-48\"><a href=\"#cb1-48\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         (<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">500<\/span>), (<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-49\"><a href=\"#cb1-49\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> vdd,c,f <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> cases:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-50\"><a href=\"#cb1-50\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x,r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>simulate(vdd,c,f)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-51\"><a href=\"#cb1-51\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    _,fine<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>simulate(vdd,c,f,step<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.001<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-52\"><a href=\"#cb1-52\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(np.array(r[:<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>fine[:<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]))<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-53\"><a href=\"#cb1-53\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    valid <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> r[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>vdd <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> r[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>vdd<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-54\"><a href=\"#cb1-54\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(vdd,c,f,<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(v,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> r[:<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]),valid)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The results are rounded to two to five decimal places.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare evaluated just before the start of the respective next input\ntransition.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the energy in the eighth period, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis its average input power at the supply pin.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-9\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(V)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(fF)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(MHz)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(V)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(V)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(fJ)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(µW)<\/th>\n<th>Level test<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.20000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">28.80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.88<\/td>\n<td>Pass<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.20000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">72.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.20<\/td>\n<td>Pass<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">500<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.09818<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.10182<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">60.84<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30.42<\/td>\n<td>Pass<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.80000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12.80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.28<\/td>\n<td>Pass<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">500<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.04097<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.75903<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11.56<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.78<\/td>\n<td>Pass<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.05723<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.14277<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">26.42<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">26.42<\/td>\n<td>Pass<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">500<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.21528<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.58642<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">15.11<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.55<\/td>\n<td>Fail<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.30717<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.89290<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">36.68<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">36.68<\/td>\n<td>Fail<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_051_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Output voltage traces at 500 MHz for 1.2 V with 50 fF and 0.8 V with 20 or 50 fF, normalised to the supply voltage, with level limits.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Normalized output swings at 500 MHz show the effect\nof supply voltage and load capacitance; the 0.8 V, 50 fF case fails the\nillustrated level requirements.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interpretation and Design Decision<\/h2>\n<p>At 100 MHz, both the 0.8 V and the 1.2 V variants with 20 fF reach\nalmost the full voltage swing. Their power ratio is therefore\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>1.2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.444<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8^2/1.2^2=0.444<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a higher frequency or with a larger load, the available charging and\ndischarging time becomes decisive. The 0.8 V case with 50 fF at 500 MHz\nconsumes less power than the corresponding 1.2 V variant but fails the\nsignal-level requirement. The lower energy per period here is partly a\nconsequence of incomplete charging and discharging, not an equivalent\nimprovement in efficiency.<\/p>\n<p>For the example specification of 500 MHz and 50 fF, 1.2 V is the\nworking choice among the operating points tested. At 0.8 V, the load\nwould have to be reduced or the effective drive strength increased for\nthis frequency. Another option would be to lower the frequency. The\neight cases do not establish an exact maximum frequency; a finer\nparameter search including a margin would be necessary for that.<\/p>\n<p>Repeating the calculation with approximately 1 ps instead of 2 ps\nchanges the first four output quantities by less than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin their respective units. The energy balance error is below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfJ in both calculations. For the two cases that fail, the difference\nbetween the initial and final voltages of the eighth period is less than\n1 mV; it is not exactly zero. The energy balance accounts for this\nresidual change. The good numerical agreement verifies only the\nsimplified model and its solution. Real process variation, temperature,\nparasitic coupling, input drivers, supply voltage drops and actual\ntiming conditions remain unaddressed. Chip implementation would require\na process-specific SPICE model, layout extraction and tests across\nprocess, voltage and temperature corners (PVT).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the third table row below the ideal full-swing value of 72 fJ?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The output only traverses approximately 0.098\nto 1.102 V. The charge transferred from the supply is smaller; a small\noverlap current is also possible.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At 0.8 V, 50 fF and 500 MHz, what is the maximum permissible low\nlevel?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> 0.08 V. The calculated value of 0.21528 V\nclearly misses the target. The high level is also below 0.72 V.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which two independent checks reveal an integration error?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Step-size comparison and energy conservation;\nboth check numerical consistency. A separate experiment with real\nhardware would be necessary to assess model validity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the code, double the capacitance to 40 fF at 0.8 V and 500 MHz.\nWhat is to be expected?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The slower voltage change causes the voltage\nswing to deteriorate; the levels must be checked using the new\nsimulation. Proportional scaling of the entire waveform would not be\ncorrect for the same period.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Digital\ncircuit and analogue interface<\/a>; the next specialisation module M15\ncovers embedded systems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW 6.012 – Lectures 14–16: CMOS and switching delay<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lectures\n14–16: switching delay, power, scaling; lecture table only. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://computationstructures.org/notes/cmos/notes.html\">Steve\nWard – Computation Structures, Chapter 6: CMOS<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.1 and § 6.5:\ncapacitive switching and voltage dependence of power; not evidence of a\nSPICE simulation. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
